Thinking: We need to answer using the skill. Provide explanation that it's possible using maplibre-contour library, steps, code snippets, considerations. Also mention DEM source encoding, setup, adding source, layer, customizing thresholds, performance. Provide full example. Also note that maplibre doesn't have built-in contour generation. Provide alternative if not possible. Provide references.

## Short answer  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on the fly** from a `raster-dem` source with the **`maplibre‑contour`** library.  
The library runs a Web‑Worker that reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hill‑shade, color‑relief or 3D terrain, builds a vector tile of contour features, and feeds it to MapLibre as a normal `vector` source. No pre‑generated contour tileset is required.

Below is a complete, step‑by‑step guide (including a minimal working example) that shows how to:

1. **Add your DEM source** (`raster-dem`).  
2. **Create a `DemSource`** from `maplibre‑contour`.  
3. **Register the protocol URL** that the worker will serve.  
4. **Add a vector source** that points at that protocol URL.  
5. **Add a line layer** (or a label layer) that draws the contours.  
6. **Tune the contour spacing** per‑zoom with the `thresholds` option.  

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** (or the latest stable) | `map.setTerrain`, `color‑relief`, `hillshade` etc. are all supported, and the style spec for vector sources is stable. |
| **A raster‑DEM source** (`type: "raster-dem"`) that you already use for hillshade/terrain. The DEM must be encoded exactly as you tell `maplibre‑contour` (e.g. `terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`). |
| **`maplibre-contour`** npm package (or a bundled copy) | Provides the `DemSource` class that does the heavy lifting in a Web‑Worker. |
| **A modern browser** with Web‑Worker and `OffscreenCanvas` support (all current browsers have this). |

```bash
# If you use a bundler (webpack, vite, etc.)
npm install maplibre-gl maplibre-contour
```

If you prefer a plain‑HTML page, you can pull the UMD build from a CDN:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

---

## 2. Add the DEM source (the same one you’ll use for terrain)

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Create the DEM source – this is the *source of truth* for elevation.
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // Example: a Terrarium‑encoded DEM hosted on a tile server
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  // Must match the encoding you’ll tell maplibre‑contour
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Optional: limit maxzoom to avoid fetching tiles you never need
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already have `map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem'})` or a hillshade layer, keep that code – the contour generation will reuse the same tiles.

---

## 3. Create a `DemSource` (the contour generator)

```javascript
// 2️⃣  Import the library (ESM syntax)
import { DemSource } from 'maplibre-contour';

// 3️⃣  Build a DemSource that knows how to read your DEM tiles.
const demSource = new DemSource({
  // Same URL pattern you used for the raster‑dem source
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the DEM encoding (terrarium, mapbox, raw)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom level you expect to request contours for.
  // The worker will request DEM tiles up to this zoom.
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true to run the worker in a separate thread (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 4️⃣  Hook the DemSource into MapLibre – this registers the custom protocol.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

`setupMaplibre` registers a **protocol handler** (by default `mlcontour://`) that the MapLibre style can point to. The worker will fetch DEM tiles, compute contour lines, and return them as a vector tile in Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) format.

---

## 4. Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the protocol URL

```javascript
// 5️⃣  Add a vector source that uses the custom protocol.
// The URL is generated by the DemSource for a given set of options.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is a *template* – the worker will replace {z}/{x}/{y}
  // with the tile coordinates it needs.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour generation options ────────────────────────
      //  • thresholds: per‑zoom spacing (minor, major) in meters
      //  • elevationKey: name of the property that will hold the elevation value
      //  • levelKey: name of the property that will hold the contour “level”
      //  • contourLayer: an arbitrary name for the source‑layer inside the MVT
      thresholds: {
        // zoom : [minorSpacing, majorSpacing]
        8:  [200, 1000],
        10: [100, 500],
        12: [50, 200],
        14: [20, 100]
      },
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // will be a numeric value (meters)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 1 = major, 0 = minor (you can style differently)
      contourLayer: 'contours' // name of the source‑layer inside the vector tile
    })
  ],
  // The worker can generate tiles up to this zoom; keep it in sync with DemSource.maxzoom
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**What the options mean**

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `thresholds` | A map of *zoom → [minor, major]* spacing (in metres). The worker will generate two sets of lines: “minor” (thin) and “major” (thick). Adjust to suit the scale of your map. |
| `elevationKey` | Property name that will contain the exact elevation of the line (e.g., `1500`). Useful for labeling. |
| `levelKey` | Property that tells you whether the line is a major contour (`1`) or a minor one (`0`). |
| `contourLayer` | The name of the *source‑layer* inside the generated vector tile. You’ll reference this in the `source-layer` property of your style layer. |

---

## 5. Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)

```javascript
// 6️⃣  Draw the contour lines.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // Must match the name you gave in `contourLayer`
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  paint: {
    // Use a semi‑transparent dark colour for minor lines,
    // a brighter colour for major lines.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',   // major
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.3)'      // minor
    ],
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 2,   // major = 2 px
      0.8    // minor = 0.8 px
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```javascript
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major contours
  layout: {
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation value
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#000',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import { DemSource } from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.esm.js';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,          // tilt so you can see terrain if you enable it
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 1️⃣ DEM source -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // (optional) enable 3‑D terrain so you can see the DEM under the contours
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // ---- 2️⃣ Contour generator -----------------------------------------
    const demSource = new DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // ---- 3️⃣ Virtual vector source --------------------------------------
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
          thresholds: {
            8:  [200, 1000],
            10: [100, 500],
            12: [50, 200],
            14: [20, 100]
          },
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level',
          contourLayer: 'contours'
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    // ---- 4️⃣ Contour line layer -----------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-color': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',
          'rgba(0,0,0,0.3)'
        ],
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 2,
          0.8
        ]
      }
    });

    // ---- 5️⃣ Optional label layer ---------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 12,
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser and you’ll see:

* A 3‑D terrain surface (if you kept `setTerrain`).  
* Contour lines automatically generated from the same DEM tiles.  
* Major contours labeled with their elevation.

---

## 7. Performance & Gotchas

| Issue | What to watch for | Mitigation |
|-------|-------------------|------------|
| **Worker startup cost** | The first request spawns a Web‑Worker and parses the DEM tiles. | Keep `worker: true` (default) – the worker stays alive for the map’s lifetime. |
| **Tile over‑fetch** | If `maxzoom` on the DEM source is low, the worker may need to request higher‑zoom DEM tiles to compute fine‑grained contours. | Set `maxzoom` on the DEM source at least as high as the highest zoom you’ll request contours for. |
|

