Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for turning the blank “page‑background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native to **MapLibre GL JS** – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** (the version that introduced the `sky` object) | Earlier versions have no sky support. |
| A **`raster-dem` source** that you already use for `map.setTerrain()` | The sky (and its fog) only renders when 3‑D terrain is active. |
| **Pitch > 0** (you already have `70°`) | With a top‑down view the sky is never visible. |
| The map’s **background color** can stay whatever you like – it will be hidden behind the sky. | No extra work needed. |

If any of those are missing, upgrade or add them first.

---

## 2. Add the terrain (you already have this)

```js
// Example – add the DEM source
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // <-- your DEM URL
  tileSize: 256,               // usually 256 or 512
  encoding: 'terrarium'        // match your DEM encoding
});

// Enable 3‑D terrain
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0           // tweak later if you want stronger relief
});
```

> **Tip:** Call `setTerrain()` *after* the source is added and *before* you call `setSky()`.  
> The sky will automatically pick up the terrain’s existence.

---

## 3. Turn on the sky

The sky is **not a layer** – it is a *root‑level* style object that you set with `map.setSky()`.  
All sky‑related properties are paint‑style values; you can change them at any time.

```js
// Call this after the map has finished loading (or inside map.on('load', …))
map.setSky({
  // 1️⃣ Overall sky colour – a light blue works well for a clear day
  'sky-color': '#87CEEB',          // sky‑blue

  // 2️⃣ How the sky blends into the horizon line (0 = hard edge, 1 = very soft)
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

  // 3️⃣ Colour of the horizon itself (often a lighter version of the sky)
  'horizon-color': '#FFFFFF',      // pure white works for a bright horizon

  // 4️⃣ Fog (haze) that appears where the terrain meets the sky
  //    – colour of the fog (usually a desaturated blue or gray)
  'fog-color': '#B0C4DE',          // light steel‑blue works nicely

  // 5️⃣ How quickly the fog fades into the sky (0 = no fog, 1 = thick fog)
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

  // 6️⃣ How the fog fades into the ground (0 = no ground fog, 1 = thick ground fog)
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Typical range | Visual effect |
|----------|---------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | any CSS colour | Base colour of the dome above the horizon. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | 0 – 1 | Softness of the transition from sky to horizon line. |
| `horizon-color` | any CSS colour | Colour of the thin line where sky meets terrain. |
| `fog-color` | any CSS colour | Colour of the atmospheric haze that “fills” the space between ground and sky. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | 0 – 1 | How much the fog mixes with the horizon colour (larger = more fog near the horizon). |
| `fog-ground-blend` | 0 – 1 | How much the fog mixes with the terrain (larger = thicker ground fog). |

You can experiment with the numbers until the look feels right for your map style.  
A common “clear‑day” preset is the one shown above; for a sunset you might use a deeper `sky-color` (`#FFB347`) and a warm `fog-color` (`#FFCC99`).

---

## 4. Optional: Dynamically change the sky (e.g., day/night)

Because the sky is just a set of style properties, you can update it on the fly:

```js
function setDaySky() {
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#FFFFFF',
    'fog-color': '#B0C4DE',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
  });
}

function setNightSky() {
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#0B1E3F',          // dark navy
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.7,
    'horizon-color': '#1A2B4C',
    'fog-color': '#2C3E50',          // deep gray‑blue fog
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.6,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });
}

// Example: switch at 18:00 local time
const hour = new Date().getHours();
(hour >= 18 || hour < 6) ? setNightSky() : setDaySky();
```

---

## 5. Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D Terrain + Sky + Fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true     // improves 3‑D rendering
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // match your DEM
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 3️⃣ Add a hillshade layer (optional – gives you shaded relief under the sky)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });

    // 4️⃣ Set the sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#FFFFFF',
      'fog-color': '#B0C4DE',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it locally) and you should see:

* 3‑D terrain draped with a gentle hillshade,
* a blue dome above the horizon,
* a soft white horizon line,
* a light bluish haze that fades into the ground.

---

## 6. Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears (still blank page background).** | • `map.setTerrain()` not called *or* terrain source missing.<br>• MapLibre version < 5.6.<br>• `pitch` is `0`. | • Verify the DEM source loads (check network tab).<br>• Upgrade to the latest MapLibre.<br>• Set `pitch` > 0 (you already have 70°). |
| **Fog looks like a solid colour covering the whole map.** | `fog-ground-blend` or `horizon-fog-blend` set to `1`. | Reduce those values (e.g., `0.3`‑`0.5`). |
| **Horizon line is too sharp / “hard edge”.** | `sky-horizon-blend` is `0`. | Increase to `0.4`‑`0.7`. |
| **Sky colour looks washed out over imagery.** | The map’s background layer is opaque and sits **above** the sky. | Ensure the background layer is either removed or placed **below** the sky (background layers are always at the bottom, but a custom `background` layer with a solid colour can hide the sky). Remove or set its `paint['background-color']` to `transparent`. |
| **Performance drops dramatically when zoomed in.** | Terrain mesh is heavy *and* you have many high‑resolution DEM tiles. | • Lower `exaggeration` if you don’t need extreme relief.<br>• Use a lower‑resolution DEM (e.g., maxzoom = 12) for mobile devices.<br>• Turn off the hillshade layer if you only need the sky. |

---

