Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for putting a **pre‑rendered shaded‑relief JPEG tile set** under all of your vector data (roads, parks, labels, etc.) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1. What you are adding

* **Source type** – `raster` (not `raster-dem`).  
* **Layer type** – `raster`.  
* **Purpose** – a static picture of terrain (hillshade, hypsometric tint, …).  
* **Effect on other layers** – none. It behaves like any other raster background; you only have to make sure it is placed **below** the vector layers you want on top.

> **Important:** Because the tiles are already rendered images, you cannot use any of the elevation‑specific layer types (`hillshade`, `color‑relief`, `setTerrain()`, etc.). Those are only for *DEM* sources. Treat the JPEG tiles exactly like a normal raster basemap.

---

## 2. Minimal HTML / JS skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded‑relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (empty style, we’ll add layers later)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll fill this in below
      layers: []            // we’ll fill this in below
    },
    center: [0, 0],          // pick a sensible start location
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster‑relief source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Replace the URL template with the one that serves your JPEG tiles.
  // Example: https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
  map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
    type: 'raster',
    tiles: [
      'https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
    ],
    tileSize: 256,          // most JPEG tiles are 256 px; change if yours are 512
    // optional: if you have a TileJSON endpoint you can use `url: '…/tilejson.json'`
    // optional: `attribution: '© My Relief Tiles'`
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Add the raster layer that draws the relief
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'relief',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'relief-basemap',
    paint: {
      // If you want the relief a little translucent so underlying background shows:
      // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
      // Fade‑in time when tiles load (default 300 ms). Set to 0 for instant:
      // 'raster-fade-duration': 0
    }
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add your vector source(s) – e.g. OpenStreetMap vector tiles
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example using the free OSM vector tiles from MapTiler:
  map.addSource('osm', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/v3/tiles.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
  });

  // Roads (line layer)
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'road-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'osm',
    'source-layer': 'transportation',
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ffffff',
      'line-width': 1.5
    }
  });

  // Labels (symbol layer) – this will automatically sit on top of the roads
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'place-labels',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'osm',
    'source-layer': 'place',
    layout: {
      'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
      'text-size': 12,
      'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': '#000000',
      'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
      'text-halo-width': 1
    }
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Ensure the raster stays at the bottom
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // If you ever add more layers later, you can always move the relief layer:
  // map.moveLayer('relief', map.getStyle().layers[0].id); // puts it first
  // Or, after all layers are added, you can reorder:
  // const allIds = map.getStyle().layers.map(l => l.id);
  // map.moveLayer('relief', allIds[0]); // first in the stack

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  Optional: add a background colour for the “no‑data” area
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'background',
    type: 'background',
    paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } // light gray where no relief tiles exist
  }, 'relief'); // insert *before* the relief layer so the relief covers it

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 7️⃣  Done – the map will now show your JPEG hillshade under the vector data.
  // -------------------------------------------------
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each block does

| Step | What it adds | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1** | Empty style → you control the order of every layer | Guarantees the relief is the first raster layer |
| **2** | `raster` source named `relief-basemap` | Points MapLibre at your JPEG tiles |
| **3** | `raster` layer `relief` that draws the source | This is the visual basemap |
| **4** | Vector source (`osm`) + line + symbol layers | Your actual data (roads, labels, etc.) |
| **5** | `moveLayer` (optional) | Guarantees the relief stays at the bottom even if you later add more layers |
| **6** | `background` layer (optional) | Gives a colour for areas where no relief tiles exist (e.g., ocean) |
| **7** | All set! | The relief appears under everything else |

---

## 3. Quick checklist for a smooth integration

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Source URL** | Verify the tile URL works directly in a browser (e.g., `https://mytileserver.com/relief/10/512/384.jpg`). |
| **Tile size** | Most JPEG tiles are 256 px; if yours are 512 px set `tileSize: 512`. |
| **CORS** | The tile server must send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can read the images. |
| **Attribution** | Add an `attribution` field to the source or a separate `symbol` layer if required by the data license. |
| **Opacity / Fade** | Use `raster-opacity` (0 – 1) and `raster-fade-duration` to blend the relief with a background colour or to avoid a “pop‑in” effect when tiles load. |
| **Layer order** | The relief layer must be **below** any vector layers you want on top. Use `map.moveLayer()` if you add layers later. |
| **Zoom limits** | If your relief tiles only exist up to a certain zoom, set `maxzoom` on the source (e.g., `maxzoom: 12`). MapLibre will then fall back to lower‑zoom tiles when you zoom further. |
| **Performance** | Raster tiles are cheap to draw, but if you have many vector layers on top, consider simplifying them (e.g., `line-width` ≤ 2, `symbol` text‑halo) for low‑end devices. |

---

## 4. Common “gotchas” & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Relief tiles appear gray/blank** | Wrong URL template, missing `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders, or CORS block. | Open a tile URL directly in the browser; check network console for 403/404. |
| **Relief shows but labels are hidden** | The relief layer was added **after** the label layer, so it sits on top. | Call `map.moveLayer('relief', 'place-labels')` (or any layer that should be above it) or add the relief **first**. |
| **Relief looks too bright/dark** | The JPEG already contains a colour ramp you don’t want to modify. | Do **not** set any `raster-color` or `raster-brightness-*` paint properties – they are for raw imagery, not pre‑shaded relief. |
| **Tiles flicker when panning** | Default `raster-fade-duration` (300 ms) causes a fade‑in each time a new tile loads. | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` in the layer’s `paint`. |
| **Ocean area is white** | No relief tiles for ocean, and no background colour. | Add a `background` layer (see step 6) or set `map.setStyle({...})` with a `background-color`. |
| **Map is very slow at high zoom** | You have both the relief raster and many high‑detail vector layers. | Reduce vector layer detail (e.g., filter out small features at high zoom) or use a lower‑resolution relief (set `maxzoom` on the source). |

---

## 5. Optional enhancements

### 5.1. Add a subtle **hillshade overlay** on top of the JPEG

If you want a little extra depth you can stack a *second* `hillshade` layer that reads from the **same DEM** (if you also have the DEM tiles) **on top of** the JPEG. This works because the hillshade is drawn as a separate layer and can be blended with the pre‑rendered relief.

```js
// Assuming you also have a DEM source called 'terrain-dem' (type: 'raster-dem')
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade-soft',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.3,
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.35)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.25)'
  }
}, 'relief'); // insert *after* the JPEG relief but before vector layers
```

> **Caveat:** This adds extra decoding work (the DEM tiles) and will increase CPU/GPU load. Use only if you really need that extra 3‑D feel.

### 5.2. Add a **sky/fog** effect for 3‑D terrain (if you later enable `setTerrain`)

If you later decide to switch from a static JPEG to a true 3‑D terrain mesh, you can keep the same style and just call:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88c0d0',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'fog-color': '#c0d0ff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

The sky will automatically appear *behind* the raster relief, so you may want to hide the JPEG relief (`map.setLayoutProperty('relief', 'visibility', 'none')`) when you enable true terrain.

